     <h1>💊 Horse Nutritional Supplements</h1>
    <p>A well-balanced diet of good hay, pasture, and water covers most of a horse's nutritional needs. However, some horses benefit from supplements.</p>

    <h2>🤔 When Are Supplements Needed?</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>When hay quality is poor or lacks certain nutrients</li>
        <li>For competition or working horses</li>
        <li>During pregnancy or lactation</li>
        <li>For older horses with joint or digestive issues</li>
        <li>When a vet identifies a specific deficiency</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>🧪 Common Types of Supplements</h2>
    <table border="1" cellpadding="8" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; margin-top:10px;">
        <tr style="background:#e8f5e9;">
            <th>Type</th>
            <th>Purpose</th>
            <th>Examples</th>
        </tr>
        <tr><td>Vitamin supplements</td><td>Support immunity and vision</td><td>Vitamin E, A, B-complex</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mineral supplements</td><td>Support bone strength</td><td>Calcium, Phosphorus, Zinc</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Joint supplements</td><td>Reduce inflammation</td><td>Glucosamine, Chondroitin, MSM</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Digestive supplements</td><td>Support gut health</td><td>Probiotics, Prebiotics</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hoof supplements</td><td>Improve hoof quality</td><td>Biotin, Zinc, Methionine</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Coat supplements</td><td>Improve shine</td><td>Omega-3 fatty acids, flaxseed</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Electrolytes</td><td>Replace minerals lost through sweat</td><td>Salt, Sodium, Potassium</td></tr>
    </table>

    <h2>⚠️ Important Warnings</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Always consult your vet before starting any supplement</li>
        <li>More is NOT better — over-supplementing can cause toxicity</li>
        <li>Supplements are not a substitute for a good basic diet</li>


    </ul>
</asp:Content>
